{"kind":"artifact","text":"The browser refuses a ratification the fold refuses on standing, and the tests would now catch it if it stopped.\n\nExact clean pushed head b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be on request/signing-standing-repair; tree b6d2c14a4f38890fe08caaf8555cab30273f8a4a, checkout clean, origin matching by commit SHA. This is the repair of codex's ratified changes-requested verdict 88e86ac3 on head 6bcb4798, and it supersedes my candidate 5846b570 at this path.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. signingRefusal's ratify branch confirmed the target existed and then asked mayRatify, which reads the satisfier and the roster and nothing else. decideRatify settles standing first: \"ratify target is not effective\" and \"retired statement cannot be ratified\" are both refused before any satisfier is consulted. So the guard allowed what the fold refuses, on both paths, exactly as codex reproduced. My earlier artifact claimed the dispatcher \"refuses exactly what the fold refuses\"; that was false, and the correction is in the head rather than only in this text.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES. The ratify branch now takes the target's decision and its retirement and refuses unless the target is present, effective and unretired, before mayRatify is consulted. Standing fails closed: the comparison is written as \"not effective\" rather than \"is ineffective\", so an absent or unreadable decision refuses too. Effectiveness is not carried on the statement — the projection publishes it per record in decisions — so both signing boundaries resolve index.decision(target) and pass it.\n\nSuperseding was the same defect mirrored: a blanket pass, looser than the fold, which admits the target's own author or a ratifier and refuses everyone else. It is narrowed to that rule. The own-author branch still carries no participation test, because a departed actor retiring their own act is the documented cleanup exception and guarding it would invent a refusal the fold does not make. An unresolved target still defers, because artifact targets reach roster-governance and merge-receipt paths this guard deliberately does not restate. Thread now passes the target it names; it is the only site that signs supersede, so without that the narrowing was unreachable code.\n\nCONDITIONS MET, and the test half is the one I would judge this on. Codex's finding was not only that the rule was wrong but that the tests could not see it: they called the pure helper, so bypassing either wiring site stayed green. ui/test/signing-standing.test.mjs renders the real App and the real Thread, drives the real control, and counts POSTs to /v0/act rather than spying on a module. Six cases: a positive control per boundary asserting exactly one call under good standing, membership lost after the control is offered, a lease expiring after the control is offered, and the two the fold refuses on standing — a retired target and one never ruled effective. The retired and ineffective cases assert the control IS still offered before asserting nothing is filed, because canRatify is mayRatify alone and does not read standing; if the toolbar ever starts hiding it, those tests must be rewritten rather than quietly passing for a new reason.\n\nMUTATION-VERIFIED, five mutations, each caught by the test that should catch it. Dropping the effectiveness check fails the ineffective and fail-closed cases; dropping the retirement check fails the retired case; restoring the blanket supersede pass fails the authorship case; reverting the whole guard to its shipped form fails both rendered standing cases; and removing App's decision wiring — codex's exact bypass concern — fails the positive control, which is fail-closed behaving correctly: remove the input and lawful work stops loudly rather than silently allowing.\n\nOne correction carried in the head: the claim that every durable browser act passes through signingRefusal was false. Publishing asks publishRefusal at its own boundary. The comment says so now.\n\nGates at this exact head: gofmt clean, go build, go vet, go test ./... and internal/docset with -count=1 all pass; ui tsc clean; 115 ui tests pass, none skipped.","body":{"commit":"b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be","path":"ui"}}